Explanation :-
Logging System is a System which tracks the health of servers by checking their performance metrics to determine whether they are working correctly or not.

Questions asked :- Functional and Non Functional Requirements
Functional Requirements include creating a system which tracks the performance of the cpu by storing their metrics in time based database which stores using (key,value) pair which is like (time,cpu) pair and
non functional requirements include providing scalability and low latency.

High Level Arhitecture.
So, basically push and pull mechanism is used , in pull mechanism system asks the questions to server and then logs in their performance metric , and in push mechanism , the client asks the question and then logs
in their performance . And time based db is used for this . Kafka which is like a temporarty storage , is a maessage queue , can be used for providing scalability.

Core APIs
Post api is used for taking the performance metrics and logging in .

Revision
Logging system is a system which is used for storing all the logs such that user can query it easily , these are functional requirements , non functional requirements 
inlcude that the storage of the logs should take low latency and at the same time , the non functinoal requirements inlcude that the system should have low latency 
for storage , and query should also take very minimum time , and there should be low cost storage for the data which is not accessed frequently or is very old , 
first , we have log procuers which produce the logs , that log can be of any type like it can be of error type or debug type , then after that we have log collector 
which collectes all the types of logs , and then after that we have message queue in which log is stored , we make the whole process asyncrhonous to make the system 
with low latency and at the same time we want to make the system reliable such that no log is lost , after that log is processed ,
and all the unwanted logs are filtered and after that depending upon the applicaton , the log is stored , and then user can query it .
We can make the system scalable by horizontally scaling it , and failure is handled because we are using queues , and we will readd the log with exponential
backoff after any error. We are also adding indexing for making sure that we can answer the queries easily and efficiently. 

Back of the envelope estimation:-
Means assume 1 million servers , and each server produces 1kb/second , so total number of logs which are prodcues is (1million*1kb)/second.
